      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Tight junctions (TJs) represent the major barrier within the paracellular pathway between intestinal epithelial cells. Disruption of TJs leads to intestinal hyperpermeability (the so-called \"leaky gut\") and is implicated in the pathogenesis of several acute and chronic pediatric disease entities that are likely to have their origin during infancy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "This review provides an overview of evidence for the role of TJ breakdown in diseases such as systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS), inflammatory bowel disease, type 1 diabetes, allergies, asthma, and autism."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "A better basic understanding of this structure might lead to prevention or treatment of these diseases using nutritional or other means."
        }
      ]},
